          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "For people with type 2 diabetes, the recommended physical activity includes a mix of moderate-intensity and vigorous activity. Generally, it is suggested to aim for 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity or 75 minutes of vigorous activity. This can be combined with muscle-strengthening activities on at least two days a week. \n\nModerate-intensity activities are those where you can talk but not sing, like brisk walking, cycling on flat surfaces, or swimming. Vigorous activities are more intense, where you could say only a few words without pausing for breath. \n\nSpecific considerations based on individual health conditions may include avoiding high-impact activities if there are risks, like bleeding or foot ulcers, which should be discussed with a clinician (source: National diabetes association and physical activity guidance). \n\nI did not cover specific exercises or routines, and there may be further details on adjustments needed based on personal health status that should be discussed further with a healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
